      "problem": "Evaluate $\\dfrac{12}{7}\\times \\dfrac{21}{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}$:\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{4}\\times\\dfrac{21}{7}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{12}{4}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{21}{7}}_{⏟}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
